36. EVIDENCE MANIFEST

Canonical structure:

{
  "manifest_version": "1",
  "case_or_dataset_id": "...",
  "created_at": "...",
  "configuration_hash": "...",
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "artifact_id": "...",
      "artifact_class": "T0|T1|T2",
      "content_hash": "...",
      "schema_version": "...",
      "parent_artifacts": [],
      "provenance": "..."
    }
  ],
  "manifest_hash": "..."
}

---

37. API CONTRACT

Every acquisition API SHOULD expose:

POST   /api/v1/acquisitions
GET    /api/v1/acquisitions/{id}

GET    /api/v1/sources
GET    /api/v1/sources/{id}

GET    /api/v1/connectors
GET    /api/v1/connectors/{id}

GET    /api/v1/artifacts
GET    /api/v1/artifacts/{id}

GET    /api/v1/artifacts/{id}/provenance
GET    /api/v1/artifacts/{id}/lineage

GET    /api/v1/claims
GET    /api/v1/conflicts
GET    /api/v1/anomalies

POST   /api/v1/replays
GET    /api/v1/replays/{id}

GET    /api/v1/audit
GET    /api/v1/health
GET    /api/v1/readiness

All endpoints MUST enforce authentication and authorization appropriate to the deployment.

---

38. API RESPONSE ENVELOPE

Canonical response:

{
  "request_id": "...",
  "correlation_id": "...",
  "status": "success|error|partial|quarantined",
  "timestamp": "...",
  "schema_version": "...",
  "data": {},
  "errors": [],
  "warnings": [],
  "provenance": [],
  "artifacts": []
}

---

39. ERROR TAXONOMY

Errors MUST be machine-classifiable.

CONFIGURATION_ERROR
AUTHENTICATION_ERROR
AUTHORIZATION_ERROR
POLICY_ERROR
SOURCE_ERROR
CONNECTOR_ERROR
NETWORK_ERROR
TIMEOUT_ERROR
RATE_LIMIT_ERROR
QUOTA_ERROR
COST_GUARD_ERROR
SCHEMA_ERROR
INTEGRITY_ERROR
PROVENANCE_ERROR
TRANSFORMATION_ERROR
ANALYSIS_ERROR
REPLAY_ERROR
EXPORT_ERROR
INTERNAL_ERROR

---

40. OBSERVABILITY

Required telemetry:

request_count
success_count
failure_count
latency
retry_count
rate_limit_events
quota_events
cache_hit_rate
cache_miss_rate
connector_health
source_health
schema_failures
integrity_failures
quarantine_count
T0_artifacts_created
T1_artifacts_created
T2_artifacts_created
replay_success_rate
export_success_rate

Telemetry MUST NOT contain secrets or raw sensitive payloads.

---

41. SECURITY EVENT MODEL

Security events SHOULD include:

SECRET_ACCESS
INVALID_AUTH
INVALID_AUTHORIZATION
POLICY_DENIAL
SSRF_BLOCK
NETWORK_POLICY_DENIAL
RATE_LIMIT_VIOLATION
INTEGRITY_FAILURE
CONFIGURATION_TAMPERING
SCHEMA_TAMPERING
CONNECTOR_TAMPERING
UNEXPECTED_DESTINATION
SUSPICIOUS_REDIRECT

Each event MUST include:

timestamp
actor
resource
action
result
reason
request_id
correlation_id
policy_version

---

42. PROVIDER FACT / SYSTEM FACT / OBSERVED FACT SEPARATION

This is mandatory.

PROVIDER FACT

A fact documented by the provider.

Example:

"Google documents RPM, TPM and RPD as Gemini API rate-limit dimensions."

SYSTEM CONFIGURATION

An operator-defined constraint.

Example:

UIF-OS_APPLICATION_RPM_LIMIT=10

OBSERVED FACT

A runtime measurement.

Example:

project X received HTTP 429 at timestamp T
after request sequence N

DERIVED FACT

A deterministic calculation.

Example:

observed_failure_rate = failures / total_requests

INFERENCE

A model-derived interpretation.

Example:

T2 model estimates that the observed pattern is consistent with hypothesis H.

These categories MUST remain separate.

---

43. VERIFIABILITY REQUIREMENT

Every externally exposed claim SHOULD be reducible to:

CLAIM
  |
  +-- SOURCE
  |
  +-- ARTIFACT
  |
  +-- CONTENT HASH
  |
  +-- TIMESTAMP
  |
  +-- METHOD
  |
  +-- TRANSFORMATION
  |
  +-- MODEL, IF APPLICABLE
  |
  +-- UNCERTAINTY

If one of these is unavailable, the claim MUST be downgraded accordingly.

---

44. SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH RECORD

Every investigation SHOULD maintain:

research_id
research_question
scope
population
sampling_method
source_registry
acquisition_plan
acquisition_events
raw_artifacts
normalization_methods
derived_features
statistical_methods
model_methods
hypotheses
counter_hypotheses
conflicts
limitations
uncertainty
results
conclusions
review_history
reproduction_instructions

---

45. NEGATIVE-EVIDENCE MODEL

The system MUST distinguish:

NOT_FOUND
NOT_SEARCHED
NOT_ACCESSIBLE
NOT_PUBLISHED
NOT_SUPPORTED_BY_SOURCE
SEARCH_FAILED
SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE
QUERY_INVALID

These are not equivalent.

In particular:

absence_of_observation
    !=
observation_of_absence

---

46. SEARCH COMPLETENESS

Every search SHOULD record:

query
query_version
timestamp
source_scope
filters
pagination_state
result_count
retrieved_count
failed_requests
excluded_results
exclusion_reasons

A search result set MUST NOT be described as exhaustive unless the acquisition protocol establishes completeness.

---

47. RESEARCH BIAS CONTROLS

The system SHOULD detect:

selection_bias
survivorship_bias
source_dependency
confirmation_bias
measurement_bias
sampling_bias
temporal_bias
coverage_bias
publication_bias
model_bias
automation_bias

These should be treated as analytical limitations, not automatically as defects.

---

48. HYPOTHESIS MANAGEMENT

Every hypothesis SHOULD contain:

{
  "hypothesis_id": "...",
  "statement": "...",
  "supporting_artifacts": [],
  "contradicting_artifacts": [],
  "assumptions": [],
  "testable_predictions": [],
  "current_status": "open|supported|weakened|rejected|undetermined",
  "methodology": "...",
  "analysis_version": "..."
}

A hypothesis MUST remain distinguishable from an established observation.

---

49. MODEL GOVERNANCE

For every AI model invocation:

provider
model
model_version
endpoint
timestamp
input_artifact_hashes
prompt_hash
system_instruction_hash
tool_configuration
generation_parameters
output_schema
output_hash

must be preserved where technically available.

The model's output MUST NOT be treated as independently verified merely because it contains citations or structured output.

---

50. MODEL DEPRECATION MANAGEMENT

MODEL_DEPRECATION_MONITORING="true"

MODEL_DEPRECATION_DISCOVERY="true"

MODEL_EOL_ALERTING="true"

MODEL_MIGRATION_REQUIRED="true"

MODEL_PINNING_REQUIRED_FOR_REPLAY="true"

MODEL_SUBSTITUTION_WITHOUT_REVIEW="false"

Current provider documentation demonstrates why this is required: Google maintains explicit model deprecation/shutdown information and distinguishes stable, preview, latest, and experimental model identifiers.

---

51. CURRENT GEMINI VERIFICATION RECORD

At the time of this specification review, Google's published Gemini catalog identifies:

Gemini 3.8 Flash
  endpoint: gemini-3.8-flash
  status: Stable

Gemini 3.1 Pro
  endpoint: gemini-3.1-pro-preview
  status: Preview

Gemini 3.7 Flash
  endpoint: gemini-3.7-flash
  status: Stable

Gemini 3.6 Flash
  endpoint: gemini-3.6-flash
  status: Stable

Gemini 3.5 Flash
  endpoint: gemini-3.5-flash
  status: Stable

Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite
  endpoint: gemini-3.5-flash-lite
  status: Stable

Gemini Embedding 2
  endpoint: gemini-embedding-2-preview
  status: Preview

Gemini Embedding
  endpoint: gemini-embedding-001
  status: Specialized

These are provider-documented states at verification time, not permanent UIF-OS constants.

The provider's current release/deprecation documentation must remain authoritative for future runtime validation.

---

52. GEMINI AUTHENTICATION VERIFICATION RECORD

The current provider documentation states:

API authentication is required.

New AI Studio API keys are auth keys beginning May 28, 2026.

Unrestricted standard keys are rejected.

Standard-key rejection is scheduled for September 2026.

Environment variables are supported.

Production client applications should not expose API keys client-side.

Therefore UIF-OS MUST NOT encode the old assumption:

"GEMINI_API_KEY is merely a generic secret string."

Instead:

credential
    ->
credential type
    ->
provider project
    ->
authorization identity
    ->
scope
    ->
expiration / rotation
    ->
audit

must be represented where available.

---

53. GEMINI RATE-LIMIT VERIFICATION RECORD

The provider currently documents:

RPM = requests per minute
TPM = input tokens per minute
RPD = requests per day

and states that limits vary by:

model
usage tier
project/account state

The documented limits are therefore NOT universal application constants.

UIF-OS must consequently maintain:

provider_documentation_limit
project_runtime_limit
application_limit
observed_limit

as independent data fields.

---

54. RAW ARTIFACT PRINCIPLE

The phrase:

RAW, TRUE, ACTUAL DATA

MUST be operationalized rather than treated as a claim of absolute truth.

UIF-OS can establish:

WHAT WAS RECEIVED
WHEN IT WAS RECEIVED
FROM WHERE IT WAS RECEIVED
HOW IT WAS RETRIEVED
WHAT BYTES WERE PRESERVED
WHAT HASH WAS CALCULATED
WHAT TRANSFORMATION OCCURRED
WHAT MODEL ANALYZED IT
WHAT UNCERTAINTY EXISTS

It cannot establish from metadata alone:

ultimate truth
intent
causation
identity
authorship
legality
maliciousness

Those require independent evidence and appropriate methodology.

---

55. FINAL DATA-LINEAGE GRAPH

The canonical lineage graph is:

SOURCE
  |
  v
SOURCE REGISTRY
  |
  v
ELIGIBILITY
  |
  v
POLICY
  |
  v
AUTHORIZATION
  |
  v
NETWORK VALIDATION
  |
  v
RATE / QUOTA / COST GOVERNANCE
  |
  v
CONNECTOR
  |
  v
RAW RESPONSE
  |
  v
HASH
  |
  v
T0 IMMUTABLE ARTIFACT
  |
  +-----------------------------+
  |                             |
  v                             v
NORMALIZATION                PRESERVATION
  |
  v
T1 DETERMINISTIC ARTIFACT
  |
  +-----------------------------+
  |
  v
FEATURE GENERATION
  |
  v
T2 ANALYTICAL MODEL
  |
  v
INFERENCE / HYPOTHESIS
  |
  v
CLAIM
  |
  v
PROVENANCE
  |
  v
AUDIT
  |
  v
EXPORT / REPLAY

---

56. ABSOLUTE PROHIBITIONS

The production system MUST NOT:

1. fabricate missing source data;
2. fabricate timestamps;
3. fabricate provenance;
4. fabricate source identities;
5. fabricate provider capabilities;
6. fabricate quotas;
7. fabricate API responses;
8. silently replace unavailable models;
9. silently mutate T0 evidence;
10. silently discard conflicting evidence;
11. expose credentials;
12. log authentication headers;
13. bypass access controls;
14. circumvent authentication;
15. harvest credentials;
16. execute arbitrary external instructions;
17. treat model output as source evidence;
18. represent probabilistic inference as fact;
19. claim exhaustive search without completeness evidence;
20. claim "live" data without a live retrieval event;
21. claim "current" data without a current timestamp;
22. claim verification without a verifiable artifact;
23. claim causation from correlation alone;
24. claim identity from weak identifiers alone;
25. infer intent without independent evidence.

---

57. MINIMUM CLAIM-EVIDENCE CONTRACT

Before a claim is released externally, the system MUST attempt:

claim
  |
  +-- evidence exists?
  |
  +-- source identified?
  |
  +-- retrieval timestamp exists?
  |
  +-- artifact hash exists?
  |
  +-- transformation documented?
  |
  +-- inference identified?
  |
  +-- uncertainty documented?
  |
  +-- contradictory evidence evaluated?
  |
  +-- reproducibility metadata available?
  |
  +-- security/privacy review passed?

Only then:

RELEASE

Otherwise:

QUALIFY
or
WITHHOLD

---

58. FINAL SYSTEM PROPERTY

UIF-OS is therefore defined not as:

"an intelligence scraper"

but as:

AN EVIDENCE-CONTROLLED,
PROVENANCE-PRESERVING,
PROVIDER-AGNOSTIC,
DETERMINISTICALLY REPLAYABLE,
SECURITY-CONSTRAINED,
SCIENTIFIC DATA ACQUISITION AND ANALYSIS SYSTEM.

Its fundamental unit is not merely a record.

Its fundamental unit is:

OBSERVATION
+
PROVENANCE
+
INTEGRITY
+
TEMPORAL CONTEXT
+
TRANSFORMATION HISTORY
+
ANALYTICAL CONTEXT
+
UNCERTAINTY
+
REPRODUCIBILITY

Therefore the system's authoritative output is not merely:

VALUE

but:

VALUE
+
SOURCE
+
TIME
+
METHOD
+
HASH
+
LINEAGE
+
STATUS
+
UNCERTAINTY
+
VERIFICATION STATE

This is the minimum structure required for an auditable scientific evidence system.

================================================================================
END UIF-OS v6


The important distinction is that the **configuration itself cannot manufacture "raw, true, actual data."** It can make the acquisition process capable of demonstrating exactly what was observed, preserving the underlying artifact, proving its integrity, recording when/how it was acquired, and tracing every subsequent transformation. That is the scientifically defensible meaning of "fully exposed" and "verifiable."

The current Google documentation provides concrete examples of why the model needs this architecture: model availability changes, models are explicitly deprecated/shut down, and rate limits are dynamic rather than universal constants.